from unit_tests import U

U('Electromagnets',
 [('Which is a magnetic material?','Nickel.','Copper.','Aluminium.','Iron, nickel and cobalt are magnetic.'),
  ('Opposite magnetic poles…','attract.','repel.','have no effect.','N and S attract.'),
  ('Adding an iron core to a coil makes the electromagnet…','stronger.','weaker.','stop working.','Iron concentrates the field.')],
 [('Give two ways to make an electromagnet stronger, other than adding an iron core.','More turns of wire on the coil.~A bigger current.'),
  ('Describe how to use a plotting compass to find the shape of a bar magnet’s field.','Place the compass near the magnet and mark where the needle points.~Move the compass to the mark and repeat, building a line.~Join the marks and repeat from different starting points.'),
  ('Explain why electromagnets are used in scrapyard cranes instead of permanent magnets.','An electromagnet can be switched on to pick up iron and steel.~Switching it off releases the scrap where it is needed.~A permanent magnet cannot be switched off.'),
  ('Plan an investigation into how the number of coils affects the strength of an electromagnet.','Change the number of coils (e.g. 10, 20, 30, 40, 50).~Keep the current, core and wire the same.~Measure strength by counting paperclips picked up.~Repeat each test and calculate a mean, then plot a graph.')])

U('Work and machines',
 [('A force of 100 N moves an object 3 m. The work done is…','300 J.','33 J.','103 J.','W = F × d.'),
  ('The turning effect of a force is called a…','moment.','pressure.','pivot.','Moment = force × distance.'),
  ('A long spanner makes it easier to undo a nut because…','the distance from the pivot is bigger.','it is heavier.','it reduces friction.','Bigger distance, bigger moment.')],
 [('A 250 N force acts 0.4 m from a pivot. Calculate the moment.','M = 250 × 0.4.~= 100 N m.'),
  ('A 500 N child sits 1.2 m from a seesaw pivot. How far from the pivot must a 400 N child sit to balance it?','500 × 1.2 = 600 N m.~600 ÷ 400…~… = 1.5 m.'),
  ('Explain how a ramp makes it easier to lift a heavy box into a van.','The box is pushed along a longer distance.~So a smaller force is needed.~The work done (force × distance) is about the same.'),
  ('A crane lifts a 1200 N load 20 m in 40 s. Calculate the work done and explain where the energy goes.','W = 1200 × 20 = 24 000 J.~Energy is transferred to the load’s gravitational potential store.~Some energy is wasted heating the motor and surroundings.~So the crane uses more than 24 000 J in total.')])

U('Heating and cooling',
 [('Energy is transferred by heating from…','a hotter object to a colder one.','a colder object to a hotter one.','objects at the same temperature.','Heat flows from hot to cold.'),
  ('Which surface emits infrared best?','Dull black.','Shiny silver.','White gloss.','Matt black is the best emitter.'),
  ('Warm air rises because it is…','less dense.','more dense.','heavier.','This causes convection.')],
 [('Name the three ways energy can be transferred by heating.','Conduction and convection.~Radiation (infrared).'),
  ('Describe how a radiator heats a whole room.','Air next to the radiator is heated and expands, becoming less dense.~The warm air rises and cooler air sinks to replace it.~This convection current carries energy around the room.'),
  ('Explain why a vacuum flask keeps a drink hot.','The vacuum stops conduction and convection.~The silvered walls reflect infrared radiation back.~The stopper reduces energy loss by convection and evaporation.'),
  ('Explain four ways a family can reduce energy loss from their house.','Loft insulation traps air, reducing conduction and convection through the roof.~Cavity wall insulation reduces energy loss through walls.~Double glazing has a gap that reduces conduction through windows.~Draught excluders stop warm air escaping (convection).')])

U('Climate',
 [('Most of the air is…','nitrogen.','oxygen.','carbon dioxide.','About 78%.'),
  ('Which process removes carbon dioxide from the atmosphere?','Photosynthesis.','Combustion.','Respiration.','Plants take in CO₂.'),
  ('Which is a greenhouse gas?','Methane.','Nitrogen.','Oxygen.','Methane traps infrared.')],
 [('Give two human activities that increase carbon dioxide in the atmosphere.','Burning fossil fuels.~Cutting down/burning forests (deforestation).'),
  ('Describe the greenhouse effect.','The Sun’s radiation warms the Earth’s surface.~The Earth emits infrared radiation.~Greenhouse gases absorb some and send it back, keeping Earth warm.'),
  ('Explain the difference between weather and climate.','Weather is the conditions on a particular day.~Climate is the average weather over many years.~So one cold winter does not disprove climate change.'),
  ('Describe two effects of climate change and two ways to reduce it.','Rising sea levels from melting ice and warming water.~More extreme weather / changing habitats.~Use renewable energy instead of fossil fuels.~Plant trees / use public transport / insulate homes.')])

U('Earth’s resources',
 [('A rock containing enough metal to extract is an…','ore.','alloy.','element.','Ores are mined.'),
  ('Iron is extracted from iron oxide by heating with…','carbon.','oxygen.','water.','Carbon is more reactive.'),
  ('Recycling aluminium saves energy because…','extracting it by electrolysis uses lots of electricity.','aluminium is rare.','it is heavy.','Recycling uses about 5% of the energy.')],
 [('Complete: iron oxide + carbon → …','Iron.~Carbon dioxide.'),
  ('Explain why aluminium cannot be extracted by heating its ore with carbon.','Aluminium is more reactive than carbon.~Carbon cannot remove the oxygen from aluminium oxide.~So electrolysis must be used.'),
  ('Give three advantages of recycling metals.','Saves energy.~Saves finite ores.~Less mining damage / less landfill / less carbon dioxide.'),
  ('Evaluate recycling steel cans compared with making new steel.','Recycling uses less energy and fewer raw materials.~It reduces waste in landfill and damage from mining.~But cans must be collected, sorted and transported, which costs money and energy.~Overall recycling is usually better for the environment.')])

U('Evolution and biodiversity',
 [('Who developed the theory of evolution by natural selection?','Charles Darwin.','Gregor Mendel.','Isaac Newton.','Darwin published in 1859.'),
  ('Fossils are usually found in…','sedimentary rock.','igneous rock.','metamorphic rock.','Sediment buries remains.'),
  ('A species with no living members is…','extinct.','endangered.','adapted.','It has died out.')],
 [('Give two types of evidence for evolution.','Fossils.~DNA similarities / antibiotic resistance in bacteria.'),
  ('Explain why the fossil record is incomplete.','Most organisms decay before they can fossilise.~Fossils need special conditions to form.~Many fossils are destroyed or not yet found.'),
  ('Give three ways people can protect endangered species.','Nature reserves / protecting habitats.~Captive breeding in zoos.~Seed banks / laws against hunting.'),
  ('Explain how bacteria become resistant to an antibiotic by natural selection.','There is variation: a few bacteria have a gene for resistance.~The antibiotic kills the non-resistant bacteria.~Resistant bacteria survive and reproduce.~The resistant gene is passed on, so the population becomes resistant.')])

U('Inheritance',
 [('A section of DNA that codes for a characteristic is a…','gene.','chromosome.','nucleus.','Genes are on chromosomes.'),
  ('How many chromosomes are in a human egg cell?','23.','46.','92.','Sex cells have half.'),
  ('Choosing parents with useful features to breed is…','selective breeding.','natural selection.','fertilisation.','Humans choose the parents.')],
 [('What is the difference between a genotype and a phenotype?','Genotype is the alleles an organism has, e.g. Bb.~Phenotype is the feature that shows, e.g. brown eyes.'),
  ('In peas, tall (T) is dominant to short (t). Two Tt plants are crossed. Give the possible genotypes of the offspring and the chance of a short plant.','TT, Tt, Tt, tt.~Short plants are tt.~Chance = 1 in 4 (25%).'),
  ('Describe how to extract DNA from strawberries.','Mash the strawberries with washing-up liquid and salt to break cells open.~Filter the mixture.~Pour ice-cold ethanol on top; DNA appears as white strands.'),
  ('Discuss one benefit and one concern of genetic modification of crops, and of selective breeding.','GM benefit: pest resistance or extra vitamins, e.g. golden rice.~GM concern: possible effects on wildlife or genes spreading to wild plants.~Selective breeding benefit: higher yields / more milk.~Selective breeding concern: less variation, so disease can wipe out a whole crop or herd.')])

U('Waves and the EM spectrum',
 [('In a longitudinal wave, the vibrations are…','parallel to the direction of travel.','at right angles to the direction of travel.','random.','Sound is longitudinal.'),
  ('Ultrasound has a frequency…','above 20 000 Hz.','below 20 Hz.','between 20 and 2000 Hz.','Too high to hear.'),
  ('Which EM wave has the shortest wavelength?','Gamma rays.','Radio waves.','Microwaves.','Gamma has the highest frequency.')],
 [('A wave has frequency 4 Hz and wavelength 3 m. Calculate its speed.','v = f × λ = 4 × 3.~= 12 m/s.'),
  ('A sonar pulse takes 0.6 s to return from the sea bed. Sound in water travels at 1500 m/s. Calculate the depth.','Distance there and back = 1500 × 0.6 = 900 m.~Depth = 900 ÷ 2…~… = 450 m.'),
  ('Explain why ultrasound rather than X-rays is used to scan an unborn baby.','X-rays have high energy and can damage cells and DNA.~Ultrasound does not harm cells.~Ultrasound reflects from tissue boundaries to build an image.'),
  ('Describe one use and one danger of three different parts of the EM spectrum.','Radio waves: communication; little danger at normal levels.~Microwaves: cooking/phones; can heat body tissue.~Ultraviolet: tanning/sterilising; causes sunburn and skin cancer.~X-rays/gamma: seeing bones/treating cancer; can damage cells and cause cancer.')])

U('Earth structure and rocks',
 [('Which layer of the Earth is liquid?','Outer core.','Inner core.','Crust.','It is liquid metal.'),
  ('Rock formed from cooled magma is…','igneous.','sedimentary.','metamorphic.','Granite and basalt.'),
  ('Freeze–thaw weathering happens because water…','expands when it freezes.','contracts when it freezes.','dissolves rock.','Ice takes up more space.')],
 [('Name the four main layers of the Earth from the outside in.','Crust and mantle.~Outer core and inner core.'),
  ('Explain why granite has large crystals but basalt has small crystals.','Granite cooled slowly underground.~Crystals had a long time to grow.~Basalt cooled quickly at the surface, so the crystals are small.'),
  ('Describe how sedimentary rock forms.','Rocks are weathered and eroded into small pieces.~Rivers deposit the sediment in layers.~Layers are compacted and cemented together over millions of years.'),
  ('Describe how a piece of sedimentary rock could become igneous rock, naming the processes.','It is buried deep underground.~Heat and pressure may change it into metamorphic rock.~Deeper still it melts to form magma.~The magma cools and solidifies to form igneous rock.')])

U('The Universe',
 [('The Earth orbits the Sun once every…','year (about 365 days).','day.','month.','One orbit is one year.'),
  ('A light year is a unit of…','distance.','time.','speed.','The distance light travels in a year.'),
  ('A lunar eclipse happens when…','Earth’s shadow falls on the Moon.','the Moon blocks the Sun.','the Moon is new.','Earth is between the Sun and Moon.')],
 [('Put in order of size, smallest first: star, planet, galaxy, Universe.','Planet, star.~Galaxy, Universe.'),
  ('Explain what causes day and night.','The Earth spins on its axis.~One spin takes about 24 hours.~The side facing the Sun has day; the side facing away has night.'),
  ('Explain why the UK has longer days in summer than in winter.','The Earth’s axis is tilted.~In summer the northern hemisphere is tilted towards the Sun.~So the UK spends more of each spin in sunlight.'),
  ('Explain why we see different phases of the Moon over a month.','The Moon does not make its own light; it reflects sunlight.~The Sun always lights half of the Moon.~As the Moon orbits Earth, we see different amounts of the lit half.~This changes from new Moon to full Moon and back in about 28 days.')])

U('Contact forces',
 [('Friction acts…','against the direction of motion.','in the direction of motion.','only upwards.','It opposes sliding.'),
  ('A spring is 10 cm long and 16 cm when stretched. The extension is…','6 cm.','26 cm.','16 cm.','16 − 10.'),
  ('Streamlining a car reduces…','air resistance (drag).','weight.','mass.','A smooth shape cuts drag.')],
 [('Give one useful and one unwanted effect of friction.','Useful: grip / brakes / walking.~Unwanted: wear / wasted energy as heat.'),
  ('A spring extends 2 cm for every 1 N. Calculate the extension for 4.5 N and state Hooke’s law.','4.5 × 2 = 9 cm.~Extension is proportional to force…~… up to the limit of proportionality.'),
  ('Explain why a cyclist crouches low when riding fast.','Crouching reduces the area facing the air.~This reduces air resistance (drag).~So less energy is wasted and the cyclist can go faster.'),
  ('Describe how to investigate the relationship between force and extension for a spring.','Hang the spring from a clamp next to a vertical ruler and record its original length.~Add 1 N weights one at a time, recording the new length each time.~Calculate extension = new length − original length.~Plot force against extension; a straight line through the origin shows Hooke’s law.')])

U('Pressure',
 [('Pressure is calculated by…','force ÷ area.','force × area.','area ÷ force.','P = F ÷ A.'),
  ('Water pressure increases with…','depth.','height above the water.','the width of the container.','More water above presses down.'),
  ('Heating a gas in a sealed container makes its pressure…','increase.','decrease.','stay the same.','Particles hit the walls harder.')],
 [('A 900 N force acts on an area of 3 m². Calculate the pressure and give the unit.','900 ÷ 3 = 300.~Pascals (Pa) / N/m².'),
  ('A 600 N person stands on snow. Calculate the pressure with shoes of total area 0.04 m² and with skis of area 0.3 m².','Shoes: 600 ÷ 0.04 = 15 000 Pa.~Skis: 600 ÷ 0.3 = 2000 Pa.~The skis give a smaller pressure, so they sink less.'),
  ('Explain why a dam is built thicker at the bottom than at the top.','Water pressure increases with depth.~The force on the bottom of the dam is greatest.~A thicker base is needed to withstand it.'),
  ('Use the particle model to explain why a football gets harder when you pump in more air.','Air pressure is caused by particles hitting the inside walls.~Pumping adds more particles in the same volume.~The particles collide with the walls more often.~So the pressure inside increases and the ball feels harder.')])
